// Sudoku: 3 constraints per cell
// N-Queens: 3 constraints per queen (row, column, diagonals)
```

### What Are Common Mistakes?

The first mistake is returning false at the wrong place. You must return false only after trying all 9 digits and none works. The second mistake is continuing the outer loop after finding an empty cell instead of returning the result of the recursive call.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: continues to next cell even when current cell has no valid digit
for (char d = '1'; d <= '9'; d++) {
    if (isValid(board, r, c, d)) {
        board[r][c] = d;
        solve(board);  // BUG: ignores return value
    }
}
// falls through without returning false
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: returns false to trigger backtracking
for (char d = '1'; d <= '9'; d++) {
    if (isValid(board, r, c, d)) {
        board[r][c] = d;
        if (solve(board)) return true;  // propagate success
        board[r][c] = '.';              // undo on failure
    }
}
return false;  // no digit works
```

### What About Optimizing with Bitmasking?

Instead of scanning the row, column, and box for each digit, you can maintain three arrays of bitmasks that track which digits are already placed. This reduces the validity check from O(9) to O(1).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SudokuBitmask {
    private int[] rowMask = new int[9];
    private int[] colMask = new int[9];
    private int[] boxMask = new int[9];

    public boolean canPlace(int r, int c, int d) {
        int bit = 1 << d;
        int box = (r / 3) * 3 + (c / 3);
        return (rowMask[r] & bit) == 0
            && (colMask[c] & bit) == 0
            && (boxMask[box] & bit) == 0;
    }

    public void place(int r, int c, int d) {
        int bit = 1 << d;
        int box = (r / 3) * 3 + (c / 3);
        rowMask[r] |= bit;
        colMask[c] |= bit;
        boxMask[box] |= bit;
    }

    public void remove(int r, int c, int d) {
        int bit = 1 << d;
        int box = (r / 3) * 3 + (c / 3);
        rowMask[r] &= ~bit;
        colMask[c] &= ~bit;
        boxMask[box] &= ~bit;
    }
}
```

### What About the Minimum Remaining Values Heuristic?

Instead of scanning cells left to right and top to bottom, the MRV heuristic picks the empty cell with the fewest valid candidates first. This dramatically reduces the search space for hard puzzles because the most constrained cell is filled first, which prunes more branches early.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MRVHeuristic {
    // Find the empty cell with fewest valid digits
    public static int[] findMostConstrained(char[][] board) {
        int minOptions = 10;
        int[] bestCell = null;
        for (int r = 0; r < 9; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < 9; c++) {
                if (board[r][c] == '.') {
                    int options = countValidDigits(board, r, c);
                    if (options < minOptions) {
                        minOptions = options;
                        bestCell = new int[]{r, c};
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return bestCell;
    }

    private static int countValidDigits(char[][] board, int r, int c) {
        int count = 0;
        for (char d = '1'; d <= '9'; d++) {
            if (SudokuSolver.isValid(board, r, c, d)) count++;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

### What About Time and Space Complexity?

The worst-case time complexity is O(9^E) where E is the number of empty cells, because each empty cell can try up to 9 digits. In practice, the constraint checks prune most candidates immediately, so real Sudoku puzzles solve in milliseconds.

The space complexity is O(E) for the recursion depth, which is at most 81.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SudokuComplexity {
    // Worst case: O(9^E) where E = number of empty cells
    // Practical: milliseconds due to heavy pruning
    // Space: O(E) recursion depth, at most 81
    // With bitmask: validity check O(1) instead of O(9)
    // With MRV: much fewer branches explored
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:

- Validate a filled Sudoku board (check all constraints without solving).
- Count the number of valid solutions for a given board.
- Generate a valid Sudoku puzzle with a unique solution.
- Extend to 16x16 Sudoku (hexadoku).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SudokuValidator {
    public static boolean isValidBoard(char[][] board) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) {
            boolean[] rowSeen = new boolean[10];
            boolean[] colSeen = new boolean[10];
            boolean[] boxSeen = new boolean[10];
            for (int j = 0; j < 9; j++) {
                // Row check
                if (board[i][j] != '.') {
                    int d = board[i][j] - '0';
                    if (rowSeen[d]) return false;
                    rowSeen[d] = true;
                }
                // Column check
                if (board[j][i] != '.') {
                    int d = board[j][i] - '0';
                    if (colSeen[d]) return false;
                    colSeen[d] = true;
                }
                // Box check
                int br = (i / 3) * 3 + j / 3;
                int bc = (i % 3) * 3 + j % 3;
                if (board[br][bc] != '.') {
                    int d = board[br][bc] - '0';
                    if (boxSeen[d]) return false;
                    boxSeen[d] = true;
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```
